{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor","id":1559012,"title":"Simulationist flexibility vs Gamist rigor","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-03-25T11:02:09-07:00","id":1559012,"title":"Simulationist flexibility vs Gamist rigor","content":"<br /> This is really just a taste question and I'm interested in polling anyone who still happens to frequent the forum.<br /> <br /> Bunch of high-falutin' thoughts about mechanics design rooted in Exalted's exception-based (card-game-like) power design and combat system rigor are stubbing their toe on the following question:<br /> <br /> <i>How important is it that combat and out-of-combat feel contiguous?</i><br /> <br /> I remember having a visceral dislike of 4e D&amp;D at first because The Rules clearly concerned themselves with two separate spaces: Combat encounters and skill checks. &quot;The rest&quot; (e.g. actions taken for narrative purposes) while not discouraged or diminished just clearly weren't the concern of the rules. Still not my favorite edition, but I've warmed to the the legitimacy of the design choice since then.<br /> <br /> In contrast I've always felt that Exalted has had a high &quot;simulationist coefficient&quot; in its systems design -- the rules are there to support the story so anything that's important to the story can be resolved within the rules if you want it to be. Some of this is residual from the most ancient conceit of the Storyteller System - whatever it is you want to do, just pick the most appropriate-feeling Attr + Ability pair, roll dice and interpret the results. This makes it pretty easy to blur the line between &quot;combat time&quot; and &quot;narrative time&quot; because the underlying rules are the same everywhere. Ex3's combat system starting pulling in the 4e D&amp;D direction by wrapping everything so tightly around the &quot;initiative as currency&quot; core, but it didn't go &quot;full mechanical rigor&quot; (to the degree that say, a board game would) either.<br /> <br /> So for you all, at your tables, how important is this characteristic to your enjoyment of Exalted? How much are you willing to tolerate compartmentalization of the systems components and the narrative components if it makes the systems components more rigorous?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-25T15:12:23-07:00","id":1559020,"title":"","content":"Something I have noticed is that the compartmentalization dislike is very dependent on existing expectations.<br /> <br /> The <i>Lancer</i> RPG is built and advertised on this premise. Combat is actually directly based on D&amp;D 4e, though the non-combat rules are based on a completely different system. While the non-combat rules in Lancer are a bit light for many tastes (they put out some optional rules to beef those up a bit), and it's helped a lot by Lancer being a mecha-piloting game so one system while you're piloting a mech and a different system when you're not feels more &quot;natural,&quot; because that's how the game is sold it feels like people don't bounce off that aspect of it the same way people did with D&amp;D 4e.<br /> <br /> When you're playing a game like D&amp;D or Exalted, you have expectations about how the game works from its own history. Radical departures mechanically tend to rub people the wrong way on some level regardless of what kind of change it is. The people I play Exalted with bounced off of Essence even though Essence is closer to what they want out of Exalted because it didn't feel &quot;right&quot; compared to heavily home-brewed 2.5e or 3e does to them. Just something about Essence's alterations were too different from &quot;Exalted&quot; even if the home-brewed version was probably 80% of the way to just being Essence anyway. Starting with &quot;core&quot; Exalted felt better to them.<br /> <br /> I, for one, find myself more tolerant of going against the baseline of the &quot;core&quot; experience of games and care more about if the final results do what I want the game to do. But I also find myself the odd one out for it. So I would love a more compartmentalized Exalted if it meant a better play experience (and I think it probably would if done well), but I also know my primary gaming group would likely never touch such a thing... even if they might play an epic fantasy new TTRPG that's similar in tone and scope to Exalted but was compartmental from the get go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-25T16:00:36-07:00","id":1559021,"title":"","content":"I think Exalted invites some kind of comprehensive and elaborate system because it wants to be, and sells itself as, being a game about great cultural heroes who have a variety of avenues to have a grand and enduring impact on the world. You aren't just using skills to acquire magical items from times gone by in the ruins of great structures, you're the inheritor of the legacy of the people who forged the items and built the structures. Your interpersonal actions aren't just for the sake of overcoming specific obstacles, you're a figure in games of intrigue or social movement concerned with delving into the depths of a person's soul or shaping the attitude of nations.<br /> <br /> And sure, there's an elaborate combat system to engage with the fact that it draws inspiration and is intended to appeal to people who like stories of high-flying kung fu action, with an undercoat of pulp fiction grit.<br /> <br /> Frankly, I think that within this model, even Third Edition has not quite gone far enough. I'm somebody who still believes that bureaucracy should not have its own dedicated subsystem, but I think there's an extent to which it and several other Abilities still aren't quite living up to that premise to the same extent that the others are. It wants to use Investigation to be Sherlock Holmes, but hasn't quite come up with a way to make a who-dunnit a challenge with the capacity for scope or depth that feels in keeping with the other things (and same with being the person who committed the murder). It wants to have miraculous doctors, but is holding back on making injury and illness that quite feel as though they can be a challenge to such. It wants a legendary sailor who reaches into the great unknowns of the world, but the sea is not quite treacherous or dangerous enough.<br /> <br /> I think it feels more natural to expand the game in the direction of &quot;make the system more engaging on all of its fronts&quot; than there is for simplification, because I feel as though simplification either leaves several facets of the premise less rewarding than other ones are, or trends in the direction of giving the Exalted fewer options to be impressive and accomplished.<br /> <br /> If it was the latter, I think it would want to significantly revise what the Exalted are in the world. Not necessarily a bad premise in the interests of consistency, although one I would personally be a hard sell on and that I think would turn a lot of people away.<br /> <br /> I'd say a lot of other games get by on the scope of what their premise expects being a lot more focused.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-25T18:57:41-07:00","id":1559023,"title":"","content":"<br /> Thank you OGs, you didn't disappoint.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559020#post1559020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The <i>Lancer</i> RPG is built and advertised on this premise. Combat is actually directly based on D&amp;D 4e, though the non-combat rules are based on a completely different system. While the non-combat rules in Lancer are a bit light for many tastes (they put out some optional rules to beef those up a bit), and it's helped a lot by Lancer being a mecha-piloting game so one system while you're piloting a mech and a different system when you're not feels more &quot;natural,&quot; because that's how the game is sold it feels like people don't bounce off that aspect of it the same way people did with D&amp;D 4e.</div> </div> </div> </div>Super helpful observation. If we were playing something like X-Wing or Armada, Battletech, or even something like Undaunted, it wouldn't feel weird if the rules were light-to-nonexistent for the interpersonal stuff between missions. It's understood that the interpersonal stuff is &quot;connective tissue&quot; to create a campaign mode with stakes. In D&amp;D/Exalted, your character is your avatar and you expect them to do all the things people do, and it's jarring when they turn into a &quot;ship&quot; that you pilot during combat.<br /> <br /> The history there is interesting too; D&amp;D itself started from the premise, &quot;what if minis wargame, but your 'army' was one guy who got better over time?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559020#post1559020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I, for one, find myself more tolerant of going against the baseline of the &quot;core&quot; experience of games and care more about if the final results do what I want the game to do. But I also find myself the odd one out for it. So I would love a more compartmentalized Exalted if it meant a better play experience (and I think it probably would if done well), but I also know my primary gaming group would likely never touch such a thing... even if they might play an epic fantasy new TTRPG that's similar in tone and scope to Exalted but was compartmental from the get go.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah this is kind of where I'm going. Obviously the hard part is &quot;better play experience&quot;. For me, being mechanically rigorous (by which I mean: requires/expects few to no judgement calls to adjudicate outcomes) makes for a better play experience, but from previous conversations here, I know some people consider that a feature rather than a bug. So... shrug emoji.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isastor Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Frankly, I think that within this model, even Third Edition has not quite gone far enough. I'm somebody who still believes that bureaucracy should not have its own dedicated subsystem, but I think there's an extent to which it and several other Abilities still aren't quite living up to that premise to the same extent that the others are. It wants to use Investigation to be Sherlock Holmes, but hasn't quite come up with a way to make a who-dunnit a challenge with the capacity for scope or depth that feels in keeping with the other things (and same with being the person who committed the murder). It wants to have miraculous doctors, but is holding back on making injury and illness that quite feel as though they can be a challenge to such. It wants a legendary sailor who reaches into the great unknowns of the world, but the sea is not quite treacherous or dangerous enough.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is totally fair; I also wonder if this particular challenge is inevitable for a game with so little restriction on what <i>kind</i> of amazing person your avatar is. Combat is fine as a common denominator because physical confrontation is accepted as just kind of universal. But for all these other verticals, introducing a character with that specialty is what begets challenges in that specialty - the sea isn't treacherous enough for an Eclipse captain to be impressive in the same way that a pack of thugs isn't scary enough for a Dawn warrior to be impressive; but taking the Eclipse to sail Kimbery feels more forced than taking the Dawn to fight Ligier. If you've <i>got</i> that Eclipse, sailing Kimbery makes them look cool, sure, but if you <i>didn't</i> have that Eclipse everyone knows that whole challenge would just get replaced by something else; with the Dawn it's &quot;good thing you're here, that fight would have been way harder without you&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isastor Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bI think it feels more natural to expand the game in the direction of &quot;make the system more engaging on all of its fronts&quot; than there is for simplification, because I feel as though simplification either leaves several facets of the premise less rewarding than other ones are, or trends in the direction of giving the Exalted fewer options to be impressive and accomplished.<br /> <br /> If it was the latter, I think it would want to significantly revise what the Exalted are in the world. Not necessarily a bad premise in the interests of consistency, although one I would personally be a hard sell on and that I think would turn a lot of people away.</div> </div> </div> </div>So what I'm thinking about isn't really simplification -- if anything it's more complexity to the end of greater rigor. My benchmark for exception-driven design is Magic: the Gathering, where <i>everything</i> is rigorously deterministic and there are as near as possible <i>no</i> ambiguities, though that does mean you sometimes need a DCI judge, it's not to arbitrate, it's because you and your opponent might have insufficient expertise. I wouldn't want to get as complicated as M:tG mind you, just that degree of determinism.<br /> <br /> In practice I think it's something like creating a few (?):<br /> -- tightening up a few specific combat areas (like delays)<br /> -- deepening the &quot;project&quot; rules (incl Charms) to put systems around <i>any</i> long-horizon effort (maybe Craft should go here?)<br /> -- social is tricky, TBH I don't have a good strategy here<br /> -- maybe introducing a short-term, non-combat &quot;overcome obstacle&quot; framework that's got more play than regular/extended rolls?<br /> <br /> <br /> Will think on this some more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-25T19:49:55-07:00","id":1559024,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blackwell</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559023#post1559023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But for all these other verticals, introducing a character with that specialty is what begets challenges in that specialty - the sea isn't treacherous enough for an Eclipse captain to be impressive in the same way that a pack of thugs isn't scary enough for a Dawn warrior to be impressive; but taking the Eclipse to sail Kimbery feels more forced than taking the Dawn to fight Ligier. If you've <i>got</i> that Eclipse, sailing Kimbery makes them look cool, sure, but if you <i>didn't</i> have that Eclipse everyone knows that whole challenge would just get replaced by something else; with the Dawn it's &quot;good thing you're here, that fight would have been way harder without you&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that comparison quite touches on what I mean.<br /> <br /> It's more like... whatever you use to model another fighter (and you can model armies in Creation to be sufficient challenges for mighty Exalted in a similar manner to what Ligier is), there's a sense of elaboration that both makes the process of opposing them engaging and lends a sense of credibility to what they are and do in the world that makes being able to overcome them feel consequential.<br /> <br /> I don't think it's about having a form of other sea in another plane of existence providing something that can theoretically be escalated into but is very out of the way. It's about the idea that being able to sail the sea both has engaging gameplay and a sense of challenge to it that makes an Exalted being able to overcome feel impressive and consequential.<br /> <br /> It's the kind of thing that I've often thought in terms of &quot;all of the Abilities should feel like excelling in them made contributions to the wonders of the First Age&quot;. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-25T20:58:07-07:00","id":1559025,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blackwell</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559023#post1559023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In D&amp;D/Exalted, your character is your avatar and you expect them to do all the things people do, and it's jarring when they turn into a &quot;ship&quot; that you pilot during combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>The funny thing, at least IME, is that in Lancer your mech remains very much your avatar because it's the largest source of player expression in the game. Though the fact that there's an in-universe distinction still does help. I'd imagine if Exalted worked a bit more like some tokusatsu/manga/anime/etc. where there's a transformation/power-up sequence it might help a bit in terms of easing people from one mechanical paradigm and into another. Some sort of narrative cue of &quot;we're switching modes,&quot; signals the players to better understand the changes in expectations that can happen.<br /> <br /> While this would be a major enough re-imagining of Exalted's mechanics to justify a new edition, you could repurpose Caste/Aspect marks for this. If instead of being something that largest exists to influence some Exalted to be cautious with their powers in specific situations and other Exalted to have something simple to flex, activating your mark was the Exalted version of a Power Ranger Morph or Goku going [insert current transformation of choice] where you can really start breaking out your Essence heavy powers would be a relatively simple way to capture that sort of mental reinforcement. There's still plenty of work to do when it comes to different options. &quot;My Water Aspect mark thunders from my forehead as I take the helm because it's Sailing Time!&quot; still needs to address the issue with Sail not really being in the same realm of mechanical space as combat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah this is kind of where I'm going. Obviously the hard part is &quot;better play experience&quot;. For me, being mechanically rigorous (by which I mean: requires/expects few to no judgement calls to adjudicate outcomes) makes for a better play experience, but from previous conversations here, I know some people consider that a feature rather than a bug. So... shrug emoji. </div> </div> </div>I think the problem is that full mechanical rigor is only really possible in a highly constrained scenario. There might be more possible chess moves than atoms in the galaxy, but chess can be a mechanically rigorous game due to it's simplicity that just doesn't translate if you try for something less abstract. There's a whole debate in the kriegsspiel between OG/&quot;hard&quot; play and &quot;free&quot; play. Hard kreigsspiel is a wargame more like MtG in the sense of having very set rules with no real room for interpretation and the umpire is mostly there to ensure fair play. &quot;Free&quot; kriegsspiel unshackles the umpire specifically because kriegsspiel was meant to capture high-level command thinking and letting the umpire (who was meant to be a more experience military officer than the players) have the freedom to impose changes to the state of play meant the players had to be adaptable to the kinds of unexpected situations that can happen in real life while still in the safety of a comfy room. So even with one of the oldest wargames intentionally meant to be more than just a fun competitive exercise, there was a debate on the value of rigor vs the value of creative freedom.<br /> <br /> So, this goes back to player expectations. Good play experience is going to come from either positive reinforcement of expectations, or positive subversion of expectations (either positive by showing neutral or negative expectations to be incorrect, or just skilled expectation manipulation that helps sell things like humor, horror, political turn abouts, etc.). Mechanical rigor can only really help with positive reinforcement of expectations by ensuring outcomes that match expectations. It can actively work against the positive side of subversion (hence the whole hard vs free debate above). This has been a bane of social mechanics for a long time because social interactions are rarely so easily modeled that mechanical rigor creates positive reinforcement, and it is an arena where subversion is frequently a sought after resolution. &quot;The Fighter kills the monster with his combat skills,&quot; has a greater tolerance for mechanical repetition/rigor than, &quot;The Bard seduces everything that moves,&quot; does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In practice I think it's something like creating a few (?):<br /> -- tightening up a few specific combat areas (like delays)<br /> -- deepening the &quot;project&quot; rules (incl Charms) to put systems around <i>any</i> long-horizon effort (maybe Craft should go here?)<br /> -- social is tricky, TBH I don't have a good strategy here<br /> -- maybe introducing a short-term, non-combat &quot;overcome obstacle&quot; framework that's got more play than regular/extended rolls? </div> </div> </div>Well, to revive an old gripe of mine with 3e:<br /> <br /> 3e should have kept external and internal penalties as a default aspect of rolling. Returning to &quot;you need 10 successes at -5 dice&quot; as the actual highest non-magical bar for mundane actions remains for me an important aspect of giving Exalted game design some space to breathe. 3e effectively reducing things down to 5 successes is the most you need to overcome mundane actions is one of the big things that makes it hard to give non-combat arenas some teeth even if you're resolving things with relatively simple roll schema (like Ventures).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T09:18:18-07:00","id":1559031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559025#post1559025\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> 3e should have kept external and internal penalties as a default aspect of rolling. Returning to &quot;you need 10 successes at -5 dice&quot; as the actual highest non-magical bar for mundane actions remains for me an important aspect of giving Exalted game design some space to breathe. 3e effectively reducing things down to 5 successes is the most you need to overcome mundane actions is one of the big things that makes it hard to give non-combat arenas some teeth even if you're resolving things with relatively simple roll schema (like Ventures).</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually agree with that. Would probably add a bit more to the dynamics of success adders.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T10:48:28-07:00","id":1559032,"title":"","content":"You've both got me thinking, maybe there's something to be had here in really leaning into the postapocalyptic aspects of the setting.<br /> Exalted warriors stand out in the world for their ability to reliably deal with violent threats that wouldn't be hard for a mortal to confront, but effectively impossible to survive. Where those threats appear in the setting they do shape the world, but we also accept that even though there are big monsters and villainous Exalted and rogue armies wandering the landscape, they do so infrequently or far-away enough that we can also have regular trade routes, and ordinary mortals usually don't encounter impossible challenges in their day-to-day lives.<br /> <br /> An ordinary, skilled mortal can sail from Wavecrest to Nexus, and will very likely survive. Not that such a journey isn't risky, but it's a sure enough bet for people to do it for personal gain. About the best you can hope for if you're a mortal being paid to fight a behemoth is particularly shiny grave goods in the afterlife.<br /> <br /> If, on the other hand, it was *as* impossible for a mortal to sail straight from Harborhead to Chiaroscuro as it is for a mortal to tank a behemoth, because the sea in between is beset by frequent, epic, post-apocalyptic storms that only an Exalt could reliably navigate, then suddenly having a an Eclipse sailor could feel appropriately world-altering by opening a route that didn't exist before.<br /> <br /> Which then opens the door for more mechanically rich Sailing without introducing a specialized subsystem; for the sake of argument we crib the combat mechanics and say that &quot;the storm&quot; and &quot;the ship&quot; have the equivalent of health levels and initiative, and successfully overcoming the storm means buillding up control with &quot;withering attacks&quot; so that you Decisively overcome the challenge. Which I guess just means making the combat engine the default conflict resolution system rather than the special case for physical fighting. Which as a metaphor I think stands up? Nothing says that &quot;initiative&quot; can't be generalized into &quot;situational advantage&quot;.<br /> <br /> Still riffing so this might get a little too woo-woo, but it makes me think of something like the Order-Conferring Trade Pattern. Continuing with our nautical theme, the reason the First Age had calm and peaceful seas on which mortal trade could prosper was because Exalted sailors overcoming epic challenges lowered the frequency of those epic challenges - they literally tamed the seas with their sailing skills. It's probably a good idea to not go TOO crazy overexplaining the magic science, but at least in priniciple it would be kind of like the God of Typhoons being diminished not because an Exalt punched him in the face but because the Exalt overcame his storms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T12:43:04-07:00","id":1559033,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blackwell</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559032#post1559032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which then opens the door for more mechanically rich Sailing without introducing a specialized subsystem; for the sake of argument we crib the combat mechanics and say that &quot;the storm&quot; and &quot;the ship&quot; have the equivalent of health levels and initiative, and successfully overcoming the storm means buillding up control with &quot;withering attacks&quot; so that you Decisively overcome the challenge. Which I guess just means making the combat engine the default conflict resolution system rather than the special case for physical fighting. Which as a metaphor I think stands up? Nothing says that &quot;initiative&quot; can't be generalized into &quot;situational advantage&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or maybe we reuse the combat machinery more thoroughly? Put a &quot;huge wave&quot;, a &quot;gale-force gust&quot;, and a &quot;lightning strike&quot; on the initiative track along with the players? A Decisive on the gust might cutting loose the sails so that the gust no longer poses a danger of ripping off the mast, say.<br /> <br /> This has an appealing escalation path (scales just like combat from &quot;mortal&quot; to &quot;apocalyptic&quot;), and also means you could have both sailing through a storm and fighting a sea monster happening at the same time with different characters dealing with different types of threat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T13:51:42-07:00","id":1559034,"title":"","content":"Sail might not be the best place to start because it straddles the line between combat and non-combat arenas so strongly. I don't really think there's a need for a high level change to the setting to make most Abilities have a place to do something (Lore vs Occult is probably the lest clear zoomed out place for what they do, but largely because we have both). <br /> <br /> I don't think any edition has really struggled too much on the &quot;navigating hazards&quot; side of Sail; though like I said already 3e's flattened difficulty ratings is a bit meh. And the setting basically addresses the issue of safety: There are well known and traveled sea routes that are relatively safe and predictable, but that means stopping at strongholds of either local powers or Imperial ports, with potential tariffs/taxes, attention from authorities, and slower routes. If you want to take risks you can go faster, which is generally good whether you're a merchant or a naval vessel, but the success gets harder and harder. The Scarlet Empire can project a lot of naval strength because of Exalted captains that can lead their ships along sea routes no mortal would consider taking. Exalted raiders and pirates can likewise use that prowess to avoid mortal navies. IRL world history has been decided by these sorts of things. <br /> <br /> Where Sail usually struggles for players to feel like they're playing epic badasses is naval combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T03:12:21-07:00","id":1559087,"title":"","content":"I agree with your internal/extenal penalty reversion. The &quot;model more things with the combat engine&quot; was more directly derived from:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1559012-simulationist-flexibility-vs-gamist-rigor?p=1559024#post1559024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's more like... whatever you use to model another fighter (and you can model armies in Creation to be sufficient challenges for mighty Exalted in a similar manner to what Ligier is), there's a sense of elaboration that both makes the process of opposing them engaging and lends a sense of credibility to what they are and do in the world that makes being able to overcome them feel consequential.<br /> <br /> I don't think it's about having a form of other sea in another plane of existence providing something that can theoretically be escalated into but is very out of the way. It's about the idea that being able to sail the sea both has engaging gameplay and a sense of challenge to it that makes an Exalted being able to overcome feel impressive and consequential</div> </div> </div> </div>Bringing back the broader scale for difficulties does help address the second paragraph there; make mundane challenges hard enough that you don't have to immediately escalate to supernatural means to create challenges where Exalts' higher operational ceiling matters.<br /> <br /> My combat riff was about adding room for what Isator termed &quot;elaboration&quot;; the challenge is granted weight by how elaborately it's modelled by the game systems. We do that now mostly by adding new subsystems, this says &quot;maybe we reuse the main encounter system instead&quot;. So in the end, basically it's the same idea as sometimes choosing to resolve a combat encounter as a single die-roll if it's not worth the whole rigamarole, just in reverse. The combat engine becomes the &quot;setpiece engine&quot; for whenever you want to spin any type of challenge into an encounter with mechanical weight, and pool/difficulty resolution is for the snappy beats in between","signature":"","edited":{"userid":456,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","user":"Blackwell","date":". 03-29-2026, 02:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T07:42:07-07:00","id":1559090,"title":"","content":"I think there are things in the core book that have been given attention besides the combat and social system that give a sufficient sense of mechanical elaboration without requiring a more dedicated system. To me, it's really more an example of giving the right kind of example, maybe a <i>few</i> more steps and variations in objectives leading to an ultimate end goal, and some of the larger content of how the setting is presented.<br /> <br /> Maybe even a few instances where it might be possible to use different Abilities towards similar challenges, based on differences of applicable penalties or potential end result.<br /> <br /> It's mostly just about thinking about how this system and its priorities compare against something like the first post reference to Dungeons and Dragons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}